1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 exd4 4. Nxd4 Be7 5. Be2 Nf6 6. Nc3 O-O 7. O-O Nc6 8.
Be3 Ne5 9. h3 Bd7 10. Nd5 Nxd5 11. exd5 Re8 12. Nf3 Ng6 13. c4 Bf6 14. Qc2 Qc8
15. Rad1 Bf5 16. Bd3 Bxd3 17. Qxd3 Bxb2 18. Nd4 Ne5 19. Qb3 Bxd4 20. Bxd4 b6
21. Qc3 f6 22. f4 Ng6 23. Qg3 Qf5 24. Bc3 Kf7 25. Qf2 Qd3 26. f5 Qxc3 27. fxg6+
hxg6 28. Rc1 Qe3 29. Qxe3 Rxe3 30. Rce1 Rae8 31. Kf2 Rxe1 32. Rxe1 Rxe1 33.
Kxe1 f5 34. a4 Kf6 35. Ke2 Ke5 36. Ke3 g5 37. g3 f4+ 38. gxf4+ gxf4+ 39. Kd3 g5
{Source: Glasgow Herald, 10 Jul 1937. Contributed by Gerard Killoran. Biog
note: E St John, wife of Dr FA St John (c1873-1947, also a player, obit BCM,
Jan 1948, p15). "... one of the British Ladies' team at the Stockholm Congress.
" Probate shows she is called Edith, widow of Frederick Agar St John, b 1874,
d Manchester 1947. (Ancestry.com) "Frederick Agar St.John was born on 8 Nov
1875, in Borneo, Pichincha, Ecuador... married Edith FRASER on 31 December
1907, in Manitoba. He died in 1947 at the age of 72. There is a record for an
Edith Fraser living in the right area of Canada (Wallace, Manitoba) in 1901,
born Scotland, 1882. One possible death record; Edith Winifred Bray St John,
bc 1875, d Dec 1960, Cambridge, Cambridgeshire - but it's not her (her h.
called Leopold).} 0-1

